landscape:
The field already treats self-poisoning of self-labelling detectors as a theory problem, and it already has published adaptive NIDS that we can attack. What nobody has built is a defence that stays safe against poisoning and keeps tracking drift on a constrained IoT device.

Theory. Two 2026 preprints set the ceiling:
- WARDEN/CDC (arXiv 2607.21673) models self-poisoning as a Polya urn whose admission kernel slope b acts like a reproduction number, giving a critical contamination rate pi_c. Its Theorem 15 proves that a label-free rule watching one 1-D score stream cannot tell honest drift from tail-mimicking contamination, so detection power is capped at kappa = alpha/(pi + alpha(1 - pi)).
- 'Theory of CL Against Poisoning' (2606.29841), Theorem 3.1: no regularised learner converges under frequent shifted poison. Its 1-D proof is literally an EMA mean dragged by a bias, which is our centroid.
- Together these explain our open problem 1 (poison hidden inside genuine drift) as an impossibility result rather than a bug. Any fix has to bring in side information the single stream does not contain.

Defences. Everything published either freezes or gates:
- Frozen reserve: WARDEN.
- Fixed certified box that stops updating once the box intersection is empty: LID (SaTML 2026).
- Statistic-gated stop-start adaptation: DriftXpert's OR > T trigger, NetSight and OWAD's permutation-test gates, RTTAD's 'stop when no samples remain', and T2T rollback (which throws away the honest neighbouring task too).
- Undirected slowing: EMA in Su et al. (ICLR 2025), beta-interpolation in DriftXpert.
- Robust conformal and certified methods (RPS, ICLR 2025; CDC) are batch methods that assume exchangeability.

Attacks. The literature now has:
- habituation and boiling-frog framing (AHA, CCS 2025 poster);
- adversarially induced drift against a supervised retrained NIDS (AAAI SuSS 2026);
- poisoning of the adaptation pipeline, including induced false positives through shortcut learning (Rapid Poison, ICML 2026);
- schedules provably invisible to windowed drift monitors (ESANN 2025);
- zero-mean oscillating drift that prediction-error monitors cannot see (2603.08455);
- learned pulsing attackers timed to a monitor's refresh windows (NDSS 2026);
- hijacking of on-device instance spawning (Sensors 2024).

Gaps. The TinyML on-device-learning survey (2605.31226), covering about 70 MCU works, contains no security mechanism at all. No paper in the corpus:
- uses fleet peers, signed device events or the detector's own rejection history as side information to decide what a self-learner may absorb;
- treats a stop-learning gate as an attack surface;
- states a joint safety (minimum time to compromise) plus liveness (bounded false alarms under honest drift) contract;
- reports anything on ESP32-class hardware.

Caveats. The closest novelty risks are still unread: 'The Price of Self-Calibration' (2609.23254, abstract only), Pasikhani et al. TDSC 2026, and the AHA full text. Most corpus numbers came through a summarising fetch tool, so check them against the PDFs before citing.

candidates.experiment:
(1) N-BaIoT, Danmini plus one more device.
- Start with NO signature provided.
- Mirai and BASHLITE background at low rate creates rejected modes.
- Attackers:
  (a) single-direction ramp;
  (b) a contrastive 3-5-agent poisoner, using the prototype-bank reward from the SuSS 2026 public code;
  (c) omission: no target traffic sent before compromise;
  (d) decoy-mode budget exhaustion;
  (e) pulsing timed to the budget refill;
  (f) a window-blind schedule solving W_n v = 0 (ESANN 2025, Algorithm 2) against the ring windows;
  (g) the attack hidden inside strong synthetic drift (open problem 1).
- Compare naive, freezing baselines, TOA (true signature), TOA (poisoned signature), core/ring and RMSB.

(2) CESNET-TimeSeries24. Real long-run drift, with rejected modes coming from naturally anomalous IPs.

(3) Reporting.
- Exploitability per defence: the best-response gain in time to compromise and in FPR.
- Boiling-frog threshold eps* sigmoids with Wilson CIs over a log grid of poison rates (2603.08455 protocol).
- Measured q(rho) and b for each detector (the WARDEN urn lens).

(4) ESP32. K = 8 slots x 10 dims plus Gram-Schmidt on at most 3 vectors per cycle; measure latency, RAM and energy per learning event.

verdict.how_to_differentiate:
1. Present the theorem as the adversarial, dynamic, any-gate extension of CAPTURE Theorem 2 and 2609.23254 Remark 4, citing both, not as a new indistinguishability result. The new content is:
- an attacker who controls the refusal duration tau;
- an explicit trip cost c(tau) as a function of attacker share rho;
- a bound on FPR after the attacker leaves, about 1 - Phi(z_alpha - v*tau/s);
- the formal SAFETY(rho, T) / LIVENESS(v, alpha, L) contract.
Prove the matching positive result: a refusal region that honest drift cannot reach (TOA-style directional rationing) satisfies both properties. Back it with the 85-87% vs 0.3% measurement. This is the constructive part neither CAPTURE nor WARDEN has.

2. Reframe the blinding dual. Do not pitch "CDC can be blinded"; WARDEN concedes that as a power loss. Pitch it as a cost-bounded construction: benign-looking, slightly-off points inflate Storey's pi_up, with a minimum time-to-blind as a function of rho. Then give the fix, a direction-weighted radius budget whose time-to-blind mirrors TOA's time-to-compromise. Cite Bashari et al. and Kloft & Laskov for the general mechanism.

3. Make the empirical section the main contribution: trip attacks on gates whose rules are verified, run on IoT traffic (N-BaIoT plus one other IoT dataset):
- T2T forced rollbacks;
- OWAD's shift gate, using its public KitNET demo;
- RTTAD's stop rule;
- an EATA entropy filter, explicitly the dual of Su et al.'s BLE bypass;
- the Kloft & Laskov switch-off gate as the classic baseline.
Replace the unverified LID box and CB-RBM with verified gated or reset defences: the ASR reset (ICLR 2026), Mateen (RAID 2024), and the Fernandez-Barrios promotion gate. Either drop the ACORN-IDS stream-inflation claim or restate its threat model: the attacker influences the clean split or the pseudo-labels.

4. Report liveness metrics nobody measures: FPR over L cycles after the attacker leaves under honest drift, and labelling-queue exhaustion for DriftXpert, if its code runs.

5. Before writing:
- read AHA's full text;
- search adversarial quickest-change detection (CUSUM false-alarm and delay attacks);
- search federated-learning framing attacks and data poisoning of conformal prediction;
- search classic IDS alert flooding (squealing, Stick/Snot) and cite it as the non-learning ancestor.
